To make the circle element in your CSS code look more 3D, you can apply some additional CSS properties that create a perspective effect and add shadows. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` selector:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.9); /* New property */
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
  transform: rotateY(45deg) translateZ(20px); /* New properties */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

Here's what these new properties do:

1. `filter: brightness(0.9)` - This reduces the overall brightness of the circle slightly, which helps with the illusion of depth.

2. `transform-style: preserve-3d` - This sets the transformation mode for this element and its children to 3D.

3. `transform: rotateY(45deg) translateZ(20px)` - These two transforms rotate and move the circle along the Y-axis (i.e., around the X-axis), creating a perspective effect. You can adjust the values of these transforms to suit your needs.

Note that you may need to experiment with these properties to find the right balance between depth and readability, as too much depth can make it difficult to distinguish the circle from its surroundings.

