This implementation is efficient as it uses built-in functions like `lower`, `strsplit`, and `setdiff` to process the text quickly. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the total number of words in both texts combined.

To further optimize for large amounts of text, you could consider using a disk-based storage mechanism instead of loading everything into memory at once. This involves splitting the text files into smaller chunks, processing them separately, and then combining the results. Here's how you could modify the previous code:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(textFile1, textFile2)
    chunkSize = 1e6;   % Change this to adjust the chunk size
    uniqueWords = cell(1, 0);

    [~, ~, filePos1] = fileinfo(textFile1);   % Get the start position of the first byte in the file
    [~, ~, filePos2] = fileinfo(textFile2);

    while true
        chunk1 = fileread(textFile1, chunkSize, 'byte', filePos1);
        chunk2 = fileread(textFile2, chunkSize, 'byte', filePos2);
        filePos1 = filePos1 + chunkSize;
        filePos2 = filePos2 + chunkSize;

        if isempty(chunk1) || isempty(chunk2)
            break;
        end

        words1 = lower(strsplit(chunk1));
        words2 = lower(strsplit(chunk2));
        uniqueChunk = setdiff(words1, words2);
        uniqueChunk(uniqueChunk == {}) = {};

        uniqueWords{end+1} = uniqueChunk;
    end

    uniqueWords = cat(1, uniqueWords{:});
end
```

